'pragmat'title="where failure goes".
$ The rule types: a question ('q') and a predicate ('p') can fail, an
$ action ('a') and a function ('f') always succeed, and an exit rule
$ ('e') never returns. Questions and functions change only their out
$ affixes; predicates and actions may change global variables, too.
$ Predict which calls reach the second alternative of choose.

'variable'tested=0.

'f'twice+>n+m>: add+n+n+m.

'q'choose+>n+value>:
   n>0,n<10,twice+n+value;
   20->value.

'p'allowed+>n: n<100,incr+tested.

'e'give up+>n:
   printf+"%d is too big, stop after %d tests%n"+n+tested,exit+0.

$ A failed guard tries the next alternative. A later failure exits choose.
'a'test+>n-value:
   (allowed+n;give up+n),
   (choose+n+value,printf+"%d gives %d%n"+n+value;
    printf+"%d fails%n"+n).

'root'test+-1,test+4,test+12,test+1000,test+5.
'end'
